WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.864 --> 00:00:03.770
Begin dictation, Captain's log.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.940
We've been submerged for
weeks and are only now

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.490
seeing the first signs
of the Sargasso Sea.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.342
There are far more baby sea
turtles here than I expected

00:00:12.342 --> 00:00:15.307
and the Sargassum seaweed is a singular

00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:19.474
almost yellowish shade of
green, and especially thick.

00:00:20.704 --> 00:00:24.029
I've sent my cameras out to explore.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:25.196
End dictation.

00:00:29.168 --> 00:00:31.835
(playful music)

00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:39.872
The Sargasso Sea is located here.

00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:43.275
Zoom out a little bit.

00:00:47.308 --> 00:00:50.199
It's the only sea in the
world that is not defined

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:54.711
by any land boundaries but
instead by ocean currents.

00:00:54.711 --> 00:00:56.684
And it's known for its endemic

00:00:56.684 --> 00:00:58.517
and anomalous seaweed.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.809
(playful music)

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:04.676
This is Sargassum.

00:01:04.676 --> 00:01:07.604
There is nearly 10 million
tons of it floating

00:01:07.604 --> 00:01:11.771
on the sea's surface as this
seaweed has no root system.

00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:15.536
You might recognize it
as it often washes up

00:01:15.536 --> 00:01:17.502
on Caribbean beaches.

00:01:17.502 --> 00:01:20.593
Many scientists call the
Sargasso Sea a nursery

00:01:20.593 --> 00:01:24.562
in the open ocean because
it provides food and shelter

00:01:24.562 --> 00:01:28.679
for many of the Atlantic Ocean's infants.

00:01:28.679 --> 00:01:31.064
Most common are baby
Loggerhead sea turtles

00:01:31.064 --> 00:01:35.146
who, after hatching, swim here to mature.

00:01:35.146 --> 00:01:39.229
Young eels, shrimp, crabs,
mahi-mahi and marlins

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:43.492
also maturate here in the Sargasso.

00:01:43.492 --> 00:01:45.400
Sacré bleu, what's that?

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.567
That thing floating there?

00:01:48.666 --> 00:01:51.232
(sighs) It's plastic.

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:53.219
God do we hate plastic.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:55.610
The Sargasso Sea is also home

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.061
to the North Atlantic Garbage Patch.

00:01:58.061 --> 00:02:01.355
Trash from around the planet gathers here

00:02:01.355 --> 00:02:04.217
thanks to multiple competing currents.

00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:07.263
It's a human-made maritime disaster.

00:02:07.263 --> 00:02:10.571
- That's why we only use
biodegradable plastic

00:02:10.571 --> 00:02:13.238
on this submarine and no straws.

00:02:14.858 --> 00:02:18.358
(soft instrumental music)

00:02:21.919 --> 00:02:24.371
- Look Captain, I think one of the infants

00:02:24.371 --> 00:02:26.850
has taken a liking to the ship.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.496
So he has Bryan, so he has.

00:02:31.496 --> 00:02:33.913
(soft music)

